# Dockerfile.smoke - local harness for the SHIPPED smoke specs.
#
# The specs under dist/test/bats/smoke/ are build-time assertions of a
# downstream Dockerfile `-test` stage: they run during `just docker build
# test` in a consumer repo, and nowhere else. base is the template SOURCE,
# so it has no Dockerfile of its own and no `-test` stage to build -- which
# left the specs it SHIPS with no way to be run at all. This file is that
# way: `just test smoke`.
#
# What it stands in for, from the shipped dist/dockerfile/Dockerfile:
#
#   devel        installs the entrypoint at /entrypoint.sh
#   devel-test   COPYs the wrappers + lib into /lint, flattens
#                smoke/shared/ + smoke/<stage>/ into /smoke_test/, drops
#                to a non-root USER, and asserts with `RUN bats`.
#
# Everything below mirrors those two, and
# test/bats/unit/smoke_harness_spec.bats fails when the mirror falls behind
# -- the stage's COPY set is walked and compared against this file, so a
# COPY added there has to be added here.
#
# What it deliberately does NOT reproduce, and why that is honest rather
# than convenient:
#
#   The base image. A real devel-test stage is `FROM devel`, i.e. the
#   repo's own Ubuntu image with its apt packages. Building that here means
#   scaffolding a whole consumer repo and a multi-minute apt build -- the
#   acceptance CI job's work, not a local one-command loop's. So the
#   harness is `FROM ${TEST_TOOLS_IMAGE}`, the Alpine tooling image that
#   already carries bats + bats-support + bats-assert. The specs under test
#   assert the SHIPPED wrapper / entrypoint / bats surface, none of which
#   is Ubuntu-specific; what is traded away is anything that depends on the
#   consumer's OS or its installed packages -- a repo-local
#   test/bats/smoke/ spec asserting its own tooling, most obviously, which
#   is why the per-repo overlay is not copied in.
#
#   The lint half. devel-test also runs shellcheck over the wrappers and
#   hadolint over the Dockerfile. Those are the lint LEVEL and base runs
#   them on its own sources through `just test lint`; duplicating them here
#   would put the same finding behind two entry points.
#
# Note the FROM: the shipped `-test` stage rule ("never make a -test stage
# FROM ${TEST_TOOLS_IMAGE}") is about a stage that must inherit the REAL
# environment of the artifact under test. Nothing here is under test but
# the specs themselves, and the tooling image IS the environment they need.

# No default: the tag is resolved by the entry point (`just test smoke`,
# which asks script/test/test.sh for the content-hash tag it builds), so a
# path that reaches this build without one has left that entry point.
ARG TEST_TOOLS_IMAGE
# The reference this harness is built FROM, under the name the shipped
# template gives it. The manifest below records whatever the image was
# built from, and here that is the tooling image -- so the harness has a
# BASE_IMAGE for the same reason a consumer does, rather than a second
# spelling of the same idea.
ARG BASE_IMAGE=${TEST_TOOLS_IMAGE}
# hadolint ignore=DL3006
FROM ${TEST_TOOLS_IMAGE} AS smoke

# Who runs the specs. The real stage runs as the repo's configured
# ${USER_NAME}; what matters is only that it is NOT root, because the COPYs
# below land root-owned and a spec that needs to write into /lint has to
# fail here exactly as it would there. A fixed uid well clear of the 1000
# a host developer usually has keeps the identity from accidentally
# matching anything on the host.
ARG SMOKE_USER="smokeuser"
ARG SMOKE_UID=1500
ARG SMOKE_GID=1500

RUN addgroup -g "${SMOKE_GID}" "${SMOKE_USER}" && \
    adduser -D -u "${SMOKE_UID}" -G "${SMOKE_USER}" "${SMOKE_USER}"

# ── mirrors `sys` ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The reproducibility manifest. Without it, all three specs in
# smoke/shared/reproducibility.bats hit their own
# `_skip_unless_manifest_adopted` guard and report `ok N # skip` -- a
# green `just test smoke` reporting that the one assertion of #951 that
# text cannot make (a manifest that IS written and records nothing) was
# not made here either. That guard exists for a CONSUMER image built
# before the manifest revision, which genuinely has no manifest and must
# not have an upgrade turned into a broken build; base is not that
# consumer. Two local entry points build this file -- `just test smoke`,
# and test/bats/system/smoke_harness_spec.bats under `just test system`,
# which builds it with --no-cache over a synthesized context -- and both
# would have reported a green run over three skips.
#
# What is reproduced is the sys stage's RECORD, not its commands. The
# bare `ARG BASE_IMAGE` re-declaration is verbatim and load-bearing for
# the same reason it is there: the pre-FROM ARG is FROM-scope only, and
# without this line ${BASE_IMAGE} expands to the empty string and the
# manifest records nothing -- exactly the failure the specs below exist
# to catch, which is worth reproducing faithfully in the file that
# exercises them. BASE_IMAGE_DIGEST is defaulted for the same reason the
# template defaults it, and behaves the same way: it is the only
# expression the LABEL below and the file here can share, so a
# reference-carried digest it does not repeat leaves both of them empty
# and the build carries on. Nothing here stops a build over that, which
# is the point -- test/bats/system/smoke_harness_spec.bats builds this
# file with exactly that call and expects an image out of it.
#
# The package half cannot be verbatim: the tooling image is Alpine and
# has no dpkg, so the apk database is read out in the same
# `<name><TAB><version>` shape `dpkg-query -W` prints. The spec asserts
# the shape of the record, and the record is what stands in.
ARG BASE_IMAGE
ARG BASE_IMAGE_DIGEST=""
RUN mkdir -p /usr/local/share/base && \
    _ref_digest=""; \
    case "${BASE_IMAGE}" in \
      *@sha256:*) _ref_digest="sha256:${BASE_IMAGE##*@sha256:}" ;; \
    esac; \
    { \
      echo "base_image_ref=${BASE_IMAGE}"; \
      if [ -n "${_ref_digest}" ]; then \
        echo "base_image_pin=digest"; \
      else \
        echo "base_image_pin=none"; \
      fi; \
      echo "base_image_digest=${BASE_IMAGE_DIGEST}"; \
      sed -n 's/^PRETTY_NAME=/base_os=/p' /etc/os-release; \
    } > /usr/local/share/base/base-image.env && \
    awk -F: '/^P:/ { name = $2 } /^V:/ { printf "%s\t%s\n", name, $2 }' \
      /lib/apk/db/installed > /usr/local/share/base/packages.txt

# The record's second sink, mirrored for the same reason the first one is.
# A label is invisible to any spec running INSIDE the image, so the only
# place the two can be compared for one image is a build plus a
# `docker inspect` from outside -- which is what
# test/bats/system/smoke_harness_spec.bats does with this file.
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.base.name="${BASE_IMAGE}" \
      org.opencontainers.image.base.digest="${BASE_IMAGE_DIGEST}"

# ── mirrors `devel` ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# smoke/shared/entrypoint.bats asserts /entrypoint.sh is present and
# executable. A consumer gets it from the devel stage, whose
# ENTRYPOINT_FILE points at the script/entrypoint.sh that init.sh seeded
# from this very path.
COPY --chmod=0755 dist/script/docker/runtime/entrypoint.sh /entrypoint.sh

# ── mirrors `devel-test` ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /lint: the wrappers the specs execute, plus the lib chain they source.
# Copied root-owned and NOT --chown'd, like the stage: the ownership is
# half of what makes a permission-sensitive spec meaningful.
COPY script/*.sh /lint/
COPY dist/script/docker/lib /lint/lib
COPY dist/script/docker/wrapper /lint/wrapper

# The shared test_helper does `bats_load_library bats-support` /
# `bats-assert`; without this they are not found and every spec errors in
# setup rather than failing an assertion.
ENV BATS_LIB_PATH="/usr/lib/bats"

# HOME is what the wrapper drivers resolve caches and dotfiles against.
# The real stage sets it explicitly for the same reason: docker does not
# derive it from the USER's passwd entry. Parameterised on the ARG, never
# spelled out: a concrete username in a home path is what ADR-00000024's
# home-literal lint exists to catch, and this file is inside its scope.
ENV HOME="/home/${SMOKE_USER}"

# /smoke_test: the per-stage selective COPY, flattened. shared/ runs on
# every `-test` stage; devel-test/ is this stage's own. The per-repo
# overlay (a consumer's test/bats/smoke/) has no counterpart in base --
# base ships the specs, it does not consume them.
COPY dist/test/bats/smoke/shared/     /smoke_test/
COPY dist/test/bats/smoke/devel-test/ /smoke_test/

USER "${SMOKE_USER}"

# The assertion, at BUILD time and with no `|| true`, exactly as the stage
# does it: a failing spec fails the build, which is what makes `just test
# smoke` a gate rather than a report. It also means the layer cache is the
# memo -- an unchanged spec tree over unchanged wrappers is a run that
# already passed, and touching any of them invalidates this layer.
RUN bats /smoke_test/
